.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt RFKILL 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rfkill
.Nd the switches that keep radios off
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/rfkill
.Pp
.Pa /sys/class/rfkill/rfkillN/
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A switch keeps a radio from transmitting.
Each Wi-Fi radio has one, named after the radio,
.Pa phyN ,
and numbered
.Ar N
from 0 in the order the switches come; a number is not given again after
its switch goes.
On an IdeaPad,
.Xr ideapad 4
adds the switch
.Ql ideapad_wlan ,
the firmware's Wi-Fi bit, which belongs to no radio.
A switch has two blocks: the soft block, which software sets and clears,
and the hard block, which a key or a switch of the machine sets and only
the machine clears.
The radio runs while neither is set.
.Pp
A block stops a Wi-Fi radio and takes its interface down, as Linux's does:
a scan in progress is aborted, the station leaves its network without a
frame, and the interface's
.Dv IFF_UP
falls, which
.Xr wpa_supplicant 8
reads over rtnetlink as the interface disabled, so that it stops scanning.
While a block is set the interface does not come up:
.Dv SIOCSIFFLAGS
and
.Dv RTM_NEWLINK
fail with
.Er ERFKILL .
When the last block is lifted the interface stays down until
.Xr wpa_supplicant 8 ,
told by the
.Dv RFKILL_OP_CHANGE
event, brings it up and joins its network again.
An interface brought up after that event, before the kernel has finished
lifting the block, starts its radio when it has.
.Pp
The interface is that of Linux, so that
.Xr rfkill 8 ,
util-linux's
.Nm rfkill
and
.Xr wpa_supplicant 8
work unchanged.
.Ss /dev/rfkill
The device, at Linux's numbers 10, 242, has mode 0644: anyone reads the
switches, and root changes them.
Each read gives one event, Linux's
.Vt struct rfkill_event :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct rfkill_event {
	uint32_t idx;	/* the switch's N */
	uint8_t type;	/* RFKILL_TYPE_WLAN is 1 */
	uint8_t op;	/* ADD 0, DEL 1, CHANGE 2 */
	uint8_t soft;	/* 1 while soft blocked */
	uint8_t hard;	/* 1 while hard blocked */
};
.Ed
.Pp
An open is first given an
.Dv RFKILL_OP_ADD
for each switch, in the order of their numbers, then one event for each
switch that comes, goes or changes a block.
A read takes one event, cut to the count asked for; an open that has none
waits for one, or fails with
.Er EAGAIN
under
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ,
and
.Xr poll 2
reports it readable while it has one.
An open keeps the last 32 events it has not read.
.Pp
A write of at least 7 bytes is one event:
.Dv RFKILL_OP_CHANGE
sets the soft block of switch
.Va idx
to
.Va soft
when its type is
.Va type
or
.Va type
is
.Dv RFKILL_TYPE_ALL ,
0, and
.Dv RFKILL_OP_CHANGE_ALL
sets it on every switch of
.Va type ,
or on every switch for
.Dv RFKILL_TYPE_ALL ;
a switch that comes later starts with the block
.Dv RFKILL_OP_CHANGE_ALL
last gave its type.
.Va hard
is ignored.
The write returns the bytes it took.
.Pp
.Dv RFKILL_IOCTL_MAX_SIZE ,
.Fn _IOW 'R' 2 __u32 ,
sets the size of this open's events, from 8 to 255 bytes; above 8 an event
is the extended form,
.Vt struct rfkill_event_ext ,
whose ninth byte,
.Va hard_block_reasons ,
is
.Dv RFKILL_HARD_BLOCK_SIGNAL ,
1, while the machine holds the hard block.
.Dv RFKILL_IOCTL_NOINPUT ,
.Fn _IO 'R' 1 ,
is accepted and does nothing, since no key here changes a switch by
itself.
.Ss /sys/class/rfkill/rfkillN
Each switch has a directory with these files:
.Bl -tag -width hard_block_reasons
.It Pa name
The switch's name, as
.Ql phy0 .
.It Pa type
.Ql wlan ,
.Ql bluetooth ,
.Ql ultrawideband ,
.Ql wimax ,
.Ql wwan ,
.Ql gps ,
.Ql fm
or
.Ql nfc .
.It Pa index
.Ar N .
.It Pa soft
1 while soft blocked; root writes 0 or 1.
.It Pa hard
1 while hard blocked.
.It Pa state
2 while hard blocked, else 0 while soft blocked, else 1; root writes 0 to
set the soft block and 1 to clear it.
.It Pa hard_block_reasons
.Ql 0x1
while hard blocked, else
.Ql 0x0 .
.It Pa persistent
0: no block outlasts a reboot.
.It Pa uevent
.Ev RFKILL_NAME ,
.Ev RFKILL_TYPE ,
.Ev RFKILL_STATE
and
.Ev RFKILL_HW_BLOCK_REASON ,
one to a line, as Linux writes them.
.It Pa device
For a radio's switch, a link to the radio,
.Pa /sys/class/ieee80211/phyN ,
the directory
.Pa /sys/class/net/wlanN/phy80211
leads to as well.
For a switch of no radio, such as
.Ql ideapad_wlan ,
a link to the switch's own directory.
wpa_supplicant reads this link of every Wi-Fi switch to find its radio's,
and without one it would not watch
.Pa /dev/rfkill .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Block every Wi-Fi radio, then lift the block:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
rfkill block wifi
rfkill unblock wifi
.Ed
.Pp
The same through the switch's own files:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
echo 1 > /sys/class/rfkill/rfkill0/soft
echo 0 > /sys/class/rfkill/rfkill0/soft
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
A read under
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
found no event.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal came while a read waited.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A write of fewer than 7 bytes, of an unknown type or of an operation other
than the two above; a size outside 8 to 255; a value other than 0 or 1
written to
.Pa soft
or
.Pa state .
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A buffer outside the caller's memory.
A read that faults loses its event, as on Linux.
.It Bq Er ENOTTY
Another request.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ideapad 4 ,
.Xr wlan 4 ,
.Xr iw 8 ,
.Xr rfkill 8 ,
.Xr wpa_supplicant 8
.Sh HISTORY
The device and the class are Linux's, from Linux 2.6.31.
